{"conjecture": "The decay of long-running software systems is driven by the steady accumulation of implicit invariants—unstated assumptions about how the code behaves that are never written down because they were temporarily obvious. Each locally correct change preserves the explicit contract but silently violates or narrows these undocumented invariants, increasing the space of possible regressions faster than tests can be written to cover them. Because the invariants were never articulated, no review process or local test suite can detect their erosion until the system reaches a phase transition where every new change breaks an assumption the previous change relied on."}
